# Issue tracker: {{tracker name}}

{{One paragraph, in the user's own words: where work is tracked, who writes tickets, what
the states are, and anything an agent would get wrong by assuming GitHub.}}

Skills never name a backend. They name one of the operations below and read this file to
find out how to perform it here. Where an operation has no command, describe the manual
steps; where it has no equivalent at all, write **not supported here** and say what the
skill should do instead.

## Ticket identifiers

{{How a ticket is named and referenced — `PROJ-123`, a URL, a file path. This is the form
that appears in commit messages and in `Closes …` lines, so `reviewing-changes` uses it to
find the spec behind a diff.}}

## List open tickets

_Every ticket still open, newest or highest-priority first. Used by `catch-up` to orient and
by `handoff` to reconcile a session against reality._

{{command or steps}}

## Fetch a ticket

_One ticket in full: body, acceptance criteria, comments, and its blockers. Used by
`implement` before it writes a line, and by `reviewing-changes` to find the spec._

{{command or steps}}

## Publish a ticket

_Create a new ticket from a title and a body. Used by `to-issues`._

{{command or steps}}

## Comment on a ticket

_Append a note to an existing ticket._

{{command or steps}}

## Close a ticket

_Mark a ticket done._

{{command or steps}}

## Link a sub-ticket

_Attach a child ticket to a parent, so a spec and its slices hang together. Used by
`to-issues` when a breakdown produces a parent spec._

{{command or steps, or **not supported here** — in which case say how a parent is referenced
instead, e.g. a line at the top of each child body}}

## Blocking edges

_Record that one ticket blocks another, and read back whether a ticket's blockers are still
open. `implement` refuses to start a ticket with an open blocker, so the read side matters
more than the write side._

{{command or steps, or **not supported here** — in which case say where a blocker is written
down and how a skill should read it}}

## Wayfinding operations

_Used only by `wayfinder`, which plans work too big for one session as a **map** ticket with
child **decision tickets**. Most of it reuses the operations above — attaching a child is
*Link a sub-ticket*, blocking is *Blocking edges*, resolving is *Comment* then *Close*. This
section adds only the map-specific pieces below. `wayfinder` checks this section exists before
it charts, and stops if it is missing — so if this tracker cannot express a parent/child map
with blocking and per-ticket ownership, write **not supported here** and say that wayfinding
is unavailable on this tracker, rather than leaving the pieces blank._

- **The map, and how a ticket is marked as one.** {{How the map ticket is distinguished from
  its children — a label, a type field, a naming convention. And how a child's type
  (`research` / `prototype` / `grilling` / `task`) is recorded.}}
- **Create the map.** {{How to create the map ticket and mark it as the map.}}
- **Read / update the map body.** {{How to read and overwrite the map body — the
  low-resolution index loaded once per session.}}
- **Claim a ticket.** {{How a session marks a ticket as taken before working it, so a
  concurrent session skips it — an assignee, an owner field, a status. Note whether the claim
  can be read back to detect a lost race; most trackers cannot claim atomically.}}
- **Query the frontier.** {{How to list the map's open, unblocked, unclaimed child tickets —
  the edge of the known. Usually a filter over the children by state, blockers, and owner.}}

